> API and interpretation

The original 300-update run reaches its budget without satisfying the log-alpha
stopping criterion. Its close sine fit therefore does not establish
hyperparameter convergence; the actual update plot shows the unresolved
precision changes.

Original targets contain no sampled noise. The plotted conditional Gaussian
intervals are model quantities, not an empirical calibration certificate. Kernel
scale remains a caller choice; sparse Bayesian regression does not remove all
tuning choices. Final refitting intentionally changes historical finite-budget
weights that had been paired with newer hyperparameters and then masked without
refitting.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Tipping, Sparse Bayesian Learning and the Relevance Vector Machine (2001)|https://jmlr.org/papers/v1/tipping01a.html]

